--TEST--
`Core\Taint::assertTrusted` answers a plain `string` for a tainted argument, and that answer is taken by every grammar sink rather than by the one sink a launderer normally names — `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named` makes laundering sink-named — a value safe for HTML text is not
// safe for a shell argument, so there is no generic `sanitize()` — and then
// names the one exception: the developer validated the value and needs to say
// so. Every other `Qual::Launder` row on the roster is refused at the sinks it
// did not name; this is the row that names none, so the claim is asked of
// three of `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R11's grammars across three classes rather than of one.
// A row that grew its own narrower answer still reads right on its own line
// and fails here.
tainted string $pattern = "a.b";
tainted string $template = "%d item";
tainted string $format = "n";

// The escape happens, and the declared type is the assertion: a member that
// carried the qualifier through stops the case here rather than at a sink.
string $trustedPattern = Core\Taint::assertTrusted($pattern, "the case wrote this literal itself, ten lines up");
echo $trustedPattern, "\n";

// The pattern grammar, at the member that compiles one.
Core\Regex\Pattern $p = Core\Regex::compile($trustedPattern);
echo Core\Regex::matches("a.b", $p) ? "y" : "n", "\n";
echo Core\Regex::matches("ab", $p) ? "y" : "n", "\n";

// The `printf` template, from a second class — and a second call, because the
// escape hatch is per value and never a mode the file enters.
echo Core\Str::format(Core\Taint::assertTrusted($template, "the case wrote this literal itself"), 3), "\n";

// The pack format, from a third.
echo Core\Bytes::length(Core\Bytes::pack(Core\Taint::assertTrusted($format, "the case wrote this literal itself"), 1)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
a.b
y
n
3 item
2
